<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baschurch</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baschurch is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a>. It is often identified with Eglwysau Bassa, the burial place of the 7th-century prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cynddylan">Cynddylan</a> in the Welsh poems of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canu-heledd">Canu Heledd</a>, and has been suggested as a possible centre of the kingdom of Pengwern. Nearby lies the Berth, an earthwork enclosure that was occupied in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baschurch.jpg" alt="Baschurch" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raggatt2000 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baschurch_Church_front.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
